  "explanation": "The write-up's only justification for a surviving nonunit is: \"A nonnegative integer that never increases and starts at a positive value can never reach 0\". That inference is false (a nonincreasing positive integer sequence can reach 0) and is exactly the false lemma named in the rubric. The correct reason — a move on m,n>1 outputs two numbers whose product is lcm(m,n)>1, so they cannot both be 1 — is never drawn: the identity g·(lcm/g)=lcm(m,n) appears only to compute P'=P/g, with no conclusion about nonunits. The degeneracy audit only lists special cases (m,m)↦(m,1), (m,n)↦(1,mn), not a general argument. One added sentence repairs it.",
